thank you very much for talking to us um  obviously two years  in the making this is a huge project for  you  why did you decide that a prize like  this was the  best way to to bring about the change  that you want to create  so the earthshot prize as you said has  been two years in the making  we very much felt that uh an optimistic  prize to try and turn the narrative  around  um the pessimism and the negativity  around the climate  was the best way forward to try to find  some solutions so the asteroid prize  itself is really a way  of of elevating people's voices and  solutions that may be out there  that we've never heard of or haven't had  the chance to scale up  so it's really the most prestigious  global environmental prize you can  imagine  and it is truly global we've got a prize  council that spans the entire globe  and we've got scientific experts and  panels and people who are going to  filter through  a number of these solutions to try and  really tackle some of those  greatest environmental though when the  world  challenges  at this tipping point as the scientists  describe it do we really need another  prize is that going to  bring about the change that we need we  hope so i mean the idea being that we we  very much felt that  what was needed what was missing at the  moment was very much a case of  harnessing people's optimism and  people's hope  there's been a lot of negativity and  pessimism and i think  it's it's such a big scale topic that  people themselves don't know you know  if i recycle my plastic every single day  actually how much of a big difference am  i making  and i think a lot of people out there  want to see the next level they want to  see several levels go because  this is an ambitious prize it's a it's  an ambitious target and topic we're  dealing with  and we can make the difference if we  really put our minds together and we  come together and  harness the human ingenuity and and  spirit of  finding solutions you're certainly not  the first member of the family to talk  about  this subject you've recently talked  about edinburgh and the prince of wales  and their commitment to the environment  um how much have you spoken to them  about this prize and what was their  reaction to what you're doing  so obviously as you all know my  grandfather was a bit of a trailblazer a  long time ago  and i did a lot with the wwf in nature  long before it was sort of fashionable  or noticeable to do it my father  obviously has been about climate change  for many many years  and so i felt i sort of feel there's a  bit of a family history to sort of  inherit in in terms of the environment  but  i think each generation passes on  something crucial to the next one to  follow on  and i feel that really for me to avoid  getting into and helping the environment  is a real  mistake and this is me kind of putting  my stamp on  what i can do is in my position to  really galvanize  and increase the interest and tackle  some of these issues to really  drive a decade of change to repair the  planet we've spoken before about  how sometimes it's difficult to choose  what subjects you decide to stick your  head above the parapet  about your father has been accused at  times  being too political when it comes to  this subject how concerned are you that  you might face the same criticism  i think with all things in life if you  are willing to make a difference you  have to put yourself out there and you  have to  um commit yourself and be determined and  maybe go places that you might feel  uncomfortable other people might find  uncomfortable but  really for me the prize is about  bringing people together you know it's a  team  it's a team prize and if we can really  harness everyone's ability to come  together and tackle  the earth's biggest environmental  problems then i think the prize is the  right way of doing it because you're  rewarding you're incentivizing and  you're encouraging  so you're bringing about hope and we  feel hope and optimism  as part of this team is the real the  only way we're going to actually  progress  of course absolutely i'm sure people  will challenge that question and the  more debate about it we have we welcome  that  and and like i said this is a team prize  so if there are people out there who  think they can do it better they can  help with this then please come on board  and  we'd love that the fact that you're  talking about  trying to drive some kind of optimism um  within this sphere and within the debate  how much is that  inspired by the conversations that you  have with your own children  about it because you look at the world  and actually we are in a  dire state and it is fairly depressing  why do you think it's so important  that we have that optimism and how much  do you try and bring that into the  discussions  i think to be perfectly honest i'm  struggling to keep the optimism levels  going with my own children  and that's really kind of like a self uh  honest understanding moment where you  kind of look at yourself and go am i  doing enough on this are we really at  this stage in life where  i can't be hugely optimistic and and  pleased that my children are getting so  into nature  um because you kind of worry and dread  that they're soon going to realize that  we're in a very very  dangerous and difficult time in the  environment and that and as a parent you  feel like you're letting them down  immediately  so having watched so many david  attenborough documentaries recently with  with my children they absolutely love  them  the most recent one the extinction one  actually george and i had to turn it off  we got so sad about it halfway through  and he said to me he said you know i  don't want to watch this anymore it's  just why is it why does it come to this  and you know he's seven years old and  he's asking me these questions already  he really feels it  and i think every seven-year-old out  there can relate to that so i really  feel  from an emotional point of view as well  i think every parent everyone wants to  do the best for their children and i  think  we have to have a decade of of change a  decade of repairing the planets that we  can hand it on to the next generation  and  future generations and sustain the  prosperity  for their lives too the earth shock  prize obviously spans  ten years your children be teenagers by  then  um how do you think you will gauge  whether it's been the success that you  see hope it's going to be  well we have set ourselves ambitious  earth shots each year  so we've got five earth shots per year  for 10 years  so that's basically tackling climate  nature air oceans and waste  and we through a number of panels and  experts we all agreed that this was the  the key crux areas that we had to  challenge if we can find solutions  so we're looking roughly 50 solutions  over the next 10 years that can really  turn the tide and really change  the way we deal and tackle with  environmental challenges then we would  have it would have been a success and  it'd been worth it  but as with the moonshot landings with  jfk when he you know had that  that vision to put a man on the moon it  was called the moonshot we've adapted  that to call it the earth shot to sort  of  you know harness that ingenuity idea we  very much  hope that even if we can't necessarily  change the world in 10 years time just  from the prize alone  what we do hope is just like the  moonshot landings we can you know where  they developed  cat scanners x-ray machines uh breathing  apparatus  stuff like that i think um has been  really really important to  to come out of that so we hope that  environmentally we can still produce  some really big solutions even if we  can't necessarily ourselves just as the  prize  change everything in 10 years time one  final one because i know that you've got  to go and you're very busy  um but i wanted to get your thoughts  because i actually have to father the  fritz wells  about this at the end of lockdown what  do you think that the kobit 19 pandemic  has taught us  as a world about what we can do when we  face a crisis  that actually is a global global issue  i think certainly for my generation and  and those  below i don't think we've ever faced  anything like this um obviously previous  generations have gone through the world  wars and  and times that were far tougher than  what they are now but we are going  through a certain period of time where  families are being restricted seeing  each other uh jobs are being lost  it's difficult for everybody um but the  spirit of togetherness and the  ability to fight back i think is one of  the you know one of the humans  kind of biggest elements and i do think  it's brought us closer together i think  we're all in a shared  similar situation if we can take that  forward into the environment and and  replicate that you know we found over  190 billion pounds worth to  fix and help uh the recovery through  covert  we can do the same for environment it  really isn't that difficult and  and i went to oxford and saw the  vaccination you know attempts  and watching the cross governmental  cross-country nash international kind of  uh collaborations coming together to  find a vaccine for everyone  that was hugely inspiring and it shows  you if we if we want to do it  we can do it thank you so much
 

